1
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Seeing is believing.

2
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Casper is a fantasy, comedy, family film directed by Brad Silberling.

3
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Casper is a kind young ghost who peacefully haunts a mansion in Maine.

4
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
When specialist James Harvey arrives to communicate with Casper and his
fellow spirits, he brings along his teenage daughter, Kat.

5
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Casper quickly falls in love with Kat, but their budding relationship is complicated not only by his
transparent state, but also by his troublemaking apparition uncles and their mischievous antics.

6
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Kat, played by Christina Ricci.

7
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Dr. James Harvey, played by Bill Pullman.

8
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Carrigan, played by Cathy Moriarty.

9
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:06,000
Dibs, played by Eric Idle.

10
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Casper (voice), played by Malachi Pearson.

11
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:06,000
Stinkie (voice), played by Joe Alaskey.

12
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
Casper is a 1995 American supernatural fantasy comedy film based on
the cartoon character Casper the Friendly Ghost.

13
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
It was directed by Brad Silberling, in his feature directorial
debut, and written by Sherri Stoner and Deanna Oliver.

14
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
The film stars Christina Ricci, Bill Pullman, Cathy Moriarty, and Eric Idle, with the voices of Joe Nipote,
Joe Alaskey, Brad Garrett, and the film introduction of Malachi Pearson in the title role.

15
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
The film follows the title character who peacefully haunts a mansion called Whipstaff Manor in Friendship,
Maine, meets and befriends a teenage girl named Kat Harvey (Ricci), the daughter of Dr.

16
01:04:00,000 --> 01:04:06,000
James Harvey (Pullman), a paranormal therapist who is hired to move into
Whipstaff in order to rid the mansion of its spectral inhabitants.

17
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:06,000
Filming took place from January to June of 1994.

18
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
Casper makes extensive use of computer-generated imagery (CGI) to create the ghosts, and
is the first feature film to have a fully CGI character in the lead role.

19
01:16:00,000 --> 01:16:06,000
It goes for a darker interpretation of Casper in comparison to the previous comics, cartoons and
theatrical shorts, providing the character a backstory that addresses his death.

20
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
Casper was released in cinemas on May 26, 1995, by Universal Pictures.

21
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
It received mixed reviews from critics and earned
$290.3 million on a $55 million budget.

22
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:06,000
It spawned two direct-to-video prequels from 20th Century Fox Home Entertainment—Casper: A Spirited Beginning (1997) as well as
Casper Meets Wendy (1998)—in addition to an animated television spin-off, The Spooktacular New Adventures of Casper.

23
01:32:00,000 --> 01:32:06,000
The film explores themes of friendship, afterlife,
supernatural, halloween, bullying.

24
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:06,000
The tone is lighthearted.
